The Nautilus DevOps team is embracing serverless architecture by integrating AWS Lambda into their operational tasks. They have decided to deploy a simple Lambda function that will return a custom greeting to demonstrate serverless capabilities effectively. This function is crucial for showcasing rapid deployment and easy scalability features of AWS Lambda to the team.
Create Lambda Function: Create a Lambda function named xfusion-lambda.
Runtime: Use the Runtime Python.
Deploy: The function should print the body Welcome to KKE AWS Labs!.
Status Code: Ensure the status code is 200.
IAM Role: Create and use the IAM role named lambda_execution_role.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Option 1: AWS Management Console

1. Navigate to AWS Lambda:
    Open the AWS Management Console and search for Lambda.
    Click Create function.
2.Configure Function Details:
    Choose Author from scratch.
    Function name: Enter xfusion-lambda.
    Runtime: Select Python 3.x (e.g., Python 3.12 or Python 3.11).
3. Configure IAM Role:
    Open the AWS IAM Console.
    In the left navigation pane, choose Roles, then click Create role.
    For Trusted entity type, select AWS service.
    For Use case, choose Lambda, then click Next.
    Add permissions:Search for and select AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole (this gives basic CloudWatch logging permissions).  
    Select any additional policy your Lambda function needs (e.g., AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess, AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess).Click Next.
    Enter a Role name (e.g., lambda_execution_role or my-lambda-role).
  Click Create role.
  Add Function Code:
------------------------------------------------
Scroll down to the Code source section.

Replace the contents of lambda_function.py with the following Python code:

Python
def lambda_handler(event, context):
    return {
        'statusCode': 200,
        'body': 'Welcome to KKE AWS Labs!'
    }
--------------------------------------------------

4. Deploy & Save:
    Click Deploy to save and update the function changes.
